{"type":"message","id":"3eec1e0b","parentId":"c0eee0f8","timestamp":"2026-06-25T05:35:02.992Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tooluse_NtkXVqtB0OqHtfmjh3FS5B","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"META\n- SEO Title: Trump Cancels Signing of Housing Bill With CBDC Ban\n- Meta Description: Trump cancels signing of housing bill with CBDC ban, demanding Congress pass voting legislation first. The bipartisan bill would block Fed from issuing a digital dollar until 2030.\n- URL Slug: trump-cancels-signing-housing-bill-cbdc-ban\n- Categories: Bitcoin, Policy and Regulations\n- Primary Keyword: Trump cancels signing\n- Secondary Keywords: CBDC ban, housing bill, Federal Reserve digital dollar, 21st Century ROAD to Housing Act\n\n---\n\n# Trump Cancels Signing of Housing Bill With CBDC Ban\n\nPresident Donald Trump cancels signing of a major bipartisan housing bill that would have temporarily banned the Federal Reserve from issuing a central bank digital currency, demanding Congress first pass a controversial voting rights measure. The last-minute decision halts what was expected to be a straightforward signing of the 21st Century ROAD to Housing Act, which overwhelmingly passed both chambers with veto-proof majorities.\n\nThe bill, passed 85-5 in the Senate and 358-32 in the House, included a provision barring the U.S. government from creating a [CBDC or any digital asset that is substantially similar](https://decrypt.co/371975/trump-cancels-signing-bipartisan-housing-bill-cbdc-ban) until the end of 2030. Crypto advocates and privacy proponents had celebrated the measure as a significant win against government surveillance of financial transactions.\n\n## Trump links housing bill to voting legislation\n\nTrump announced Wednesday morning via Truth Social that he would not sign the housing legislation \"until such time as we pass the desperately needed SAVE America Act.\" The president had previously stated in March that he would block other bills until Congress prioritized the Republican-backed voting measure, which would require voters to provide proof of U.S. citizenship in person to register.\n\n### Bipartisan support for housing bill\n\nThe 21st Century ROAD to Housing Act represented rare cooperation between both political parties, with Senate Banking Committee Chair Tim Scott and Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren expressing strong support. Warren acknowledged the unusual political landscape, noting that \"Congress actually passed something good.\"\n\nThe legislation aimed to address housing affordability by cutting restrictions on new construction and prohibiting Wall Street institutions from buying up residential housing supply. A ceremonial stage had already been erected at the Capitol for the signing event before Trump's abrupt cancellation, [according to Cointelegraph](https://cointelegraph.com/news/donald-trump-cancels-signing-housing-bill-cbdc-ban).\n\n### Uncertainty over veto override\n\nIf Trump vetoes the bill, Congress would need to approve it again with a two-thirds supermajority in both chambers. The initial votes cleared that threshold handily, but political dynamics could shift following the president's public opposition.\n\n## CBDC ban provision draws crypto attention\n\nThe housing bill's most significant provision for the crypto industry was the prohibition on the Federal Reserve from creating or issuing a central bank digital currency until Dec. 31, 2030. The language carved out exemptions for stablecoins, permitting \"dollar-denominated currency that is open, permissionless and private.\"\n\n### Privacy advocates claim victory\n\nConservatives and privacy advocates have long opposed a U.S. CBDC, arguing that a government-backed digital dollar could enable surveillance of private transactions. The European Union has moved forward with digital currency plans, making the American ban a notable divergence in global monetary policy.\n\n### Legislation mirrors Trump campaign rhetoric\n\nTrump campaigned on opposition to a CBDC, making the bill's CBDC ban provision broadly aligned with his stated preferences. The last-minute cancellation raises questions about whether other crypto-related legislation, including the Digital Asset Market Clarity Act, could face similar delays.\n\n## Implications for crypto market structure bills\n\nTrump's stance creates uncertainty for pending crypto legislation, including the CLARITY Act, which would establish regulatory frameworks for digital assets. The president said in May he intended to codify a \"future-proof digital asset market structure,\" but his current legislative blockade could impact that timeline.\n\n### Congress maintains override option\n\nBoth the housing bill and any vetoed crypto legislation could theoretically pass with supermajority support. The strong bipartisan margins on the housing bill suggest Congress retains leverage, though political calculations may shift as the voting rights debate intensifies.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nTrump cancels signing of the housing bill with CBDC ban, creating immediate uncertainty for crypto policy advocates who had secured a significant legislative win. The Federal Reserve digital dollar prohibition remains in legislative limbo while the president presses his voting rights agenda. Traders should monitor whether Congress attempts a veto override and how this standoff affects the broader crypto regulatory landscape, including the pending CLARITY Act.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. What did the housing bill's CBDC ban actually prohibit?**\n\nThe legislation barred the Federal Reserve from issuing or creating a central bank digital currency or any substantially similar digital asset until the end of 2030. It included exemptions for permissionless stablecoins and dollar-denominated currencies that remain private and open.\n\n**2. Can Congress override Trump's potential veto?**\n\nYes, Congress can override a presidential veto with a two-thirds majority in both the House and Senate. The housing bill initially passed with veto-proof margins, 85-5 in the Senate and 358-32 in the House, suggesting override is possible if political dynamics hold.\n\n**3. How does this affect other crypto legislation like the CLARITY Act?**\n\nTrump's decision creates uncertainty for pending crypto bills, including the Digital Asset Market Clarity Act. The president has expressed support for digital asset market structure legislation, but his current blockade on signing bills until the voting measure passes could delay progress.\n\n**4. Why did Trump cancel the signing ceremony?**\n\nTrump cancelled the signing to pressure Congress into passing the SAVE America Act, a Republican-backed bill that would require in-person proof of citizenship for voter registration. Critics argue the voting measure would disenfranchise eligible voters.\n\n**Sources:**\n- Decrypt: https://decrypt.co/371975/trump-cancels-signing-bipartisan-housing-bill-cbdc-ban\n- Cointelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/news/donald-trump-cancels-signing-housing-bill-cbdc-ban\n\n[Word Count: 915]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1782365702992}}
{"type":"message","id":"916d630c","parentId":"3eec1e0b","timestamp":"2026-06-25T05:35:47.305Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"tooluse_oeu7E7Lb3JSLne5uJrotXI","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/coinography-run-20260625-053013-11872-6040/article/raw.md","edits":[{"oldText":"- Meta Description: Trump cancels signing of housing bill with CBDC ban, demanding Congress pass voting legislation first. The bipartisan bill would block Fed from issuing a digital dollar until 2030.","newText":"- Meta Description: Trump cancels signing of housing bill with CBDC ban, demanding Congress pass voting legislation first. The bill would block a Fed digital dollar until 2030."},{"oldText":"### Uncertainty over veto override\n\nIf Trump vetoes the bill, Congress would need to approve it again with a two-thirds supermajority in both chambers. The initial votes cleared that threshold handily, but political dynamics could shift following the president's public opposition.","newText":"### Uncertainty over veto override\n\nIf Trump vetoes the bill, Congress would need to approve it again with a two-thirds supermajority in both chambers. The initial votes cleared that threshold handily, but political dynamics could shift following the president's public opposition.\n\nRepublican leadership has repeatedly emphasized that the SAVE America Act stands little chance of passage, making Trump's linkage between the housing bill and voting legislation politically risky. Democrats who supported the housing measure may resist any attempt to leverage it for voting restrictions."},{"oldText":"### Privacy advocates claim victory\n\nConservatives and privacy advocates have long opposed a U.S. CBDC, arguing that a government-backed digital dollar could enable surveillance of private transactions. The European Union has moved forward with digital currency plans, making the American ban a notable divergence in global monetary policy.","newText":"### Privacy advocates claim victory\n\nConservatives and privacy advocates have long opposed a U.S. CBDC, arguing that a government-backed digital dollar could enable surveillance of private transactions. The European Union has moved forward with digital currency plans, making the American ban a notable divergence in global monetary policy.\n\nThe ban language specifically protects decentralized options, ensuring that stablecoins and other dollar-pegged assets can continue operating without government interference. This distinction between a government-controlled CBDC and private stablecoins reflects ongoing debates about financial privacy and centralized monetary control."}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"zai.glm-5","usage":{"input":16324,"output":473,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":16797,"cost":{"input":0.016323999999999998,"output":0.0015136000000000001,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0178376}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1782365702992,"responseId":"5b5c185e-df34-49b6-bbb9-7f5421eb1e7f"}}
{"type":"message","id":"3330e673","parentId":"916d630c","timestamp":"2026-06-25T05:35:47.348Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tooluse_oeu7E7Lb3JSLne5uJrotXI","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 3 block(s) in /tmp/coinography-run-20260625-053013-11872-6040/article/raw.md."}],"details":{"diff":"  1 META\n  2 - SEO Title: Trump Cancels Signing of Housing Bill With CBDC Ban\n- 3 - Meta Description: Trump cancels signing of housing bill with CBDC ban, demanding Congress pass voting legislation first. The bipartisan bill would block Fed from issuing a digital dollar until 2030.\n+ 3 - Meta Description: Trump cancels signing of housing bill with CBDC ban, demanding Congress pass voting legislation first. The bill would block a Fed digital dollar until 2030.\n  4 - URL Slug: trump-cancels-signing-housing-bill-cbdc-ban\n  5 - Categories: Bitcoin, Policy and Regulations\n  6 - Primary Keyword: Trump cancels signing\n  7 - Secondary Keywords: CBDC ban, housing bill, Federal Reserve digital dollar, 21st Century ROAD to Housing Act\n    ...\n 27 ### Uncertainty over veto override\n 28 \n 29 If Trump vetoes the bill, Congress would need to approve it again with a two-thirds supermajority in both chambers. The initial votes cleared that threshold handily, but political dynamics could shift following the president's public opposition.\n 30 \n+31 Republican leadership has repeatedly emphasized that the SAVE America Act stands little chance of passage, making Trump's linkage between the housing bill and voting legislation politically risky. Democrats who supported the housing measure may resist any attempt to leverage it for voting restrictions.\n+32 \n 31 ## CBDC ban provision draws crypto attention\n 32 \n 33 The housing bill's most significant provision for the crypto industry was the prohibition on the Federal Reserve from creating or issuing a central bank digital currency until Dec. 31, 2030. The language carved out exemptions for stablecoins, permitting \"dollar-denominated currency that is open, permissionless and private.\"\n 34 \n 35 ### Privacy advocates claim victory\n 36 \n 37 Conservatives and privacy advocates have long opposed a U.S. CBDC, arguing that a government-backed digital dollar could enable surveillance of private transactions. The European Union has moved forward with digital currency plans, making the American ban a notable divergence in global monetary policy.\n 38 \n+41 The ban language specifically protects decentralized options, ensuring that stablecoins and other dollar-pegged assets can continue operating without government interference. This distinction between a government-controlled CBDC and private stablecoins reflects ongoing debates about financial privacy and centralized monetary control.\n+42 \n 39 ### Legislation mirrors Trump campaign rhetoric\n 40 \n 41 Trump campaigned on opposition to a CBDC, making the bill's CBDC ban provision broadly aligned with his stated preferences. The last-minute cancellation raises questions about whether other crypto-related legislation, including the Digital Asset Market Clarity Act, could face similar delays.\n 42 \n    ...","firstChangedLine":3},"isError":false,"timestamp":1782365747348}}
